<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southeast Asia Treaty Organization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as SEATO, South-East Asia Treaty Organization, Manila Pact</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> Treaty Organization was a collective defence organisation created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> Pact of September 1954 to contain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> in Southeast Asia after the French defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>. Its members were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Weakened by divisions over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and the withdrawal of Pakistan and France, it was dissolved in 1977.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southeast-asia-treaty-organization.jpg" alt="Southeast Asia Treaty Organization" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SEATO officials; SVG by w:Sn1per · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_SEATO.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Southeast Asia Collective Defense Treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> on 8 September 1954 as part of the American effort to build anti-communist collective defence treaties, aimed in this case at keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in check. Secretary of State John Foster Dulles is considered the main force behind it, and Vice President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> had advocated an Asian equivalent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">North Atlantic Treaty Organization</a>, which became the model. The organization itself was set up on 19 February 1955 at a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, where its headquarters stood. Dulles had preferred to call it the Manila Pact, to avoid identifying it publicly with NATO.</p><p>It was headed by a Secretary General, an office created in 1957, with a council of representatives and committees for economics, security and information. The first Secretary General was the Thai diplomat Pote Sarasin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Eight members joined in all: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Only the Philippines and Thailand were Southeast Asian states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> rejected joining, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> chose not to take part formally. The Geneva Agreements of 21 July 1954 barred North Vietnam, South Vietnam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> from joining military alliances, but SEATO placed them under its protection, and Cambodia rejected this in 1956. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, SEATO had no joint commands with standing forces, and its response protocol to a common danger was vague.</p><p>The members held joint training but never deployed forces. SEATO could not intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom rejected military action, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> gave unilateral support to Laos after 1962. The United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> cited membership as a reason for involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, though British and French refusal kept SEATO itself out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> withdrew in 1973, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> seceded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, and France withdrew its financial support in 1975. After a final exercise on 20 February 1976, SEATO was dissolved on 30 June 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historians have generally regarded the pact as a failure, and the British diplomat Sir James Cable called it a fig leaf for the nakedness of American policy. Its cultural and educational programmes had lasting effects. In 1959 SEATO founded the SEATO Graduate School of Engineering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, now the Asian Institute of Technology, and a Teacher Development Center in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. It set up cholera research laboratories in Bangkok and Dacca; the Dacca laboratory became a leading cholera research centre and is now the International Centre for Diarrhoeal Disease Research, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After meeting Jawaharlal Nehru and U Nu in 1954 he turned towards nonalignment rather than the <b>Southeast Asia Treaty Organization</b>, and at the Bandung Conference in April 1955 Zhou Enlai and Phạm Văn Đồng assured him that their countries would respect Cambodia's independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>An artillery-based force, Kayforce, served in the Korean War from 1950, and in 1951 New Zealand signed the ANZUS Treaty with Australia and the United States, joining the <b>South-East Asia Treaty Organization</b> in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li></ul></section>
